I read quite a few articles in this group about class differentiation,
or lack of it, in Nethack.  One solution that occurred to me is to
have two parallel systems of magic.  For shorthand, let's call them
WizardLike (WL) and PriestLike (PL), and I'll explain my idea:

Spells in the two magic systems would have limited and/or no overlap. 
PL spells would be more allied towards divination, detection,
protection and healing; and WL spells towards direct damage, object
manipulation and trickery.  However, both systems would clearly have a
full range of spells - so the PL system would include some direct
damage spells, for example.

Gaining new spells under the WL system would involve reading books. 
Wizards are studious people who like nothing better that burying
themselves in large, dusty, obscure volumes for hours on end.  WL
spells can be forgotten over time (Wizards tend to be forgetful) but
can be re-learned by re-reading the relevant book.  So it works
exactly like the current system.

The PL system would involve being granted the ability to perform a
particular spell by your god.  This would occur on level-up/from
sacrificing/from praying (delete or alter as appropriate).  Once the
ability to perform the spell is granted, it is never forgotten (the
gift of a divine being is by definition perfect - it would be
sacrilege to suggest otherwise).

I do not envision the overall number of spells expanding massively as
a result of these changes - Wizards would get less spells than they do
currently as some would be transferred to the PL system.  A few spells
might be added, or duplicated between the two systems (Identify and
Remove Curse spring to mind).

Finally, other classes would be naturally aligned to one or other
system.  A Knight would be on the PL system, a Rogue on WL, and so on.
 This makes it easy to limit the magical ability of the different
classes - a Rogue may only be able to learn WL spells of level 4 and
lower; a Healer may only be granted a maximum of 10 spells by his/her
deity.

So, there we go.  It's a little radical, it needs refinement and
further thought, but it's a start.  I'll be fascinated to read your
suggestions and reactions.

